#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:5
msgid "Thread Safety"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:7
msgid ""
"NumPy supports use in a multithreaded context via the `threading` module in "
"the standard library. Many NumPy operations release the :term:`python:GIL`, "
"so unlike many situations in Python, it is possible to improve parallel "
"performance by exploiting multithreaded parallelism in Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:12
msgid ""
"The easiest performance gains happen when each worker thread owns its own "
"array or set of array objects, with no data directly shared between threads. "
"Because NumPy releases the GIL for many low-level operations, threads that "
"spend most of the time in low-level code will run in parallel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:17
msgid ""
"It is possible to share NumPy arrays between threads, but extreme care must "
"be taken to avoid creating thread safety issues when mutating arrays that "
"are shared between multiple threads. If two threads simultaneously read from "
"and write to the same array, they will at best produce inconsistent, racey "
"results that are not reproducible, let alone correct. It is also possible to "
"crash the Python interpreter by, for example, resizing an array while "
"another thread is reading from it to compute a ufunc operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:25
msgid ""
"In the future, we may add locking to :class:`~numpy.ndarray` to make writing "
"multithreaded algorithms using NumPy arrays safer, but for now we suggest "
"focusing on read-only access of arrays that are shared between threads, or "
"adding your own locking if you need to mutation and multithreading."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:30
msgid ""
"Note that operations that *do not* release the GIL will see no performance "
"gains from use of the `threading` module, and instead might be better served "
"with `multiprocessing`. In particular, operations on arrays with ``dtype=np."
"object_`` do not release the GIL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:38
msgid "Context Local State"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:40
msgid ""
"NumPy stores user-adjustable configuration options in :py:mod:`context "
"variables <python:contextvars>`. Context variables allow *context-local "
"state*, which means each thread in a multithreaded program or task in an "
"asyncio program has its own independent configuration. This includes the "
"following state:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:45
msgid ""
"The `numpy.errstate`, which storesl floating-point error handling "
"configuration options and the ufunc buffer size settable via `numpy."
"setbufsize`. See :doc:`/reference/routines.err` and :ref:`use-of-internal-"
"buffers` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:49
msgid ""
"The `numpy.printoptions`  and all text-formatting configuration options. "
"See :ref:`text_formatting_options` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:51
msgid ""
"The memory allocator, see :ref:`data_memory` and :ref:`NEP 49 <NEP49>` for "
"more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:54
msgid ""
"State stored in a context variable is set syntactically using the ``with`` "
"statement. For example, you can update the numpy printing options state like "
"so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:57
msgid ""
">>> with np.printoptions(precision=2):\n"
"...    np.array([2.0]) / 3\n"
"array([0.67])\n"
">>> np.array([2.0]) / 3\n"
"array([0.66666667])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:66
msgid ""
"This property applies to all context-local state, not just `numpy."
"printoptions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:69
msgid "Interaction with the `threading` module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:71
msgid ""
"Before Python 3.14, new threads always start with newly initialized context "
"state. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:74
msgid ""
"$ python3.12\n"
">>> import numpy, threading\n"
">>> def print_printoptions():\n"
"...     print(numpy.get_printoptions()['precision'])\n"
">>> with numpy.printoptions(precision=2):\n"
"...     threading.Thread(target=print_printoptions).start()\n"
"8"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:84
msgid ""
"Starting in Python 3.14 a new ``thread_inherit_context`` startup "
"configuration option for Python allows opting into a new behavior where "
"context state for spawned threads behaves syntactically as one would expect "
"the above code to behave:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:89
msgid ""
"$ python3.14 -Xthread_inherit_context=1\n"
">>> import numpy, threading\n"
">>> def print_printoptions():\n"
"...     print(numpy.get_printoptions()['precision'])\n"
">>> with numpy.printoptions(precision=2):\n"
"...     threading.Thread(target=print_printoptions).start()\n"
"2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:99
msgid ""
"See `the CPython documentation <https://docs.python.org/3/using/cmdline."
"html#envvar-PYTHON_THREAD_INHERIT_CONTEXT>`_ for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:104
msgid "Free-threaded Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:108
msgid ""
"Starting with NumPy 2.1 and CPython 3.13, NumPy also has experimental "
"support for python runtimes with the GIL disabled. See https://py-free-"
"threading.github.io for more information about installing and using :py:term:"
"`free-threaded <python:free threading>` Python, as well as information about "
"supporting it in libraries that depend on NumPy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:114
msgid ""
"Because free-threaded Python does not have a global interpreter lock to "
"serialize access to Python objects, there are more opportunities for threads "
"to mutate shared state and create thread safety issues. In addition to the "
"limitations about locking of the :class:`~numpy.ndarray` object noted above, "
"this also means that arrays with ``dtype=np.object_`` are not protected by "
"the GIL, creating data races for python objects that are not possible "
"outside free-threaded python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:122
msgid ""
"Free-threaded Python has ``thread_inherit_context`` turned on by default "
"starting in Python 3.14. See :ref:`context_local` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/thread_safety.rst:126
msgid "C-API Threading Support"
msgstr ""

